  "grid_title": "BONES PEOPLE BREAK",
  "why": "Wrist (distal radius): a fall onto an outstretched hand breaks the end of the forearm bone; very common at any age. Collarbone: a fall onto the shoulder or an outstretched arm; about 5% of adult fractures. Ankle: rolling or twisting it; one of the most common breaks in adults. Hand and finger: finger and hand-bone (metacarpal) fractures are among the most common sports fractures in teens and adults. Toe: kicking something hard, catching it on furniture, or dropping something heavy on it. Forearm in kids: the most common break in children (up to half of all childhood fractures), often from a fall onto an outstretched arm. Hip: more than 300,000 people 65 and older are hospitalized for a broken hip each year in the US, usually from a fall. A limb that looks bent, or one you can't put weight on: get it checked.",
